       The  following  list  provides  a short description of the available or
       historically available filesystems in the Linux kernel.  See the kernel
       documentation for  a  comprehensive  description  of  all  options  and
       limitations.

       eerrooffss  is  the  Enhanced Read‐Only File System, stable since Linux 5.4.
              See eerrooffss(5).

       eexxtt    is an elaborate extension of the mmiinniixx filesystem.  It has  been
              completely  superseded  by  the  second  version of the extended
              filesystem (eexxtt22) and has been removed from the kernel (in Linux
              2.1.21).

       eexxtt22   is a disk filesystem that was used by Linux for fixed  disks  as
              well  as  removable  media.   The second extended filesystem was
              designed as an extension of the extended filesystem (eexxtt).   See
              eexxtt22(5).

       eexxtt33   is  a  journaling version of the eexxtt22 filesystem.  It is easy to
              switch back and forth between eexxtt22 and eexxtt33.  See eexxtt33(5).

       eexxtt44   is a set of upgrades to eexxtt33 including  substantial  performance
              and  reliability  enhancements,  plus large increases in volume,
              file, and directory size limits.  See eexxtt44(5).

       hhppffss   is  the  High  Performance  Filesystem,  used  in  OS/2.    This
              filesystem is read‐only under Linux due to the lack of available
              documentation.

       iissoo99666600
              is  a  CD‐ROM  filesystem  type  conforming  to the ISO/IEC 9660
              standard.

              HHiigghh SSiieerrrraa
                     Linux  supports  High  Sierra,  the  precursor   to   the
                     ISO/IEC  9660  standard  for  CD‐ROM  filesystems.  It is
                     automatically recognized within  the  iissoo99666600  filesystem
                     support under Linux.

              RRoocckk RRiiddggee
                     Linux  also  supports  the  System  Use  Sharing Protocol
                     records specified by the Rock Ridge Interchange Protocol.
                     They are used  to  further  describe  the  files  in  the
                     iissoo99666600   filesystem   to   a   UNIX  host,  and  provide
                     information  such  as  long  filenames,  UID/GID,   POSIX
                     permissions, and devices.  It is automatically recognized
                     within the iissoo99666600 filesystem support under Linux.

       JJFFSS    is   a   journaling  filesystem,  developed  by  IBM,  that  was
              integrated into Linux 2.4.24.

       mmiinniixx  is the filesystem used in the Minix operating system, the  first
              to  run under Linux.  It has a number of shortcomings, including
              a 64 MB partition size limit,  short  filenames,  and  a  single
              timestamp.  It remains useful for floppies and RAM disks.

       mmssddooss  is the filesystem used by DOS, Windows, and some OS/2 computers.
              mmssddooss  filenames can be no longer than 8 characters, followed by
              an optional period and 3 character extension.

       nnccppffss  is a network filesystem that supports the NCP protocol, used  by
              Novell NetWare.  It was removed from the kernel in Linux 4.17.

              To  use  nnccppffss, you need special programs, which can be found at
              ⟨ftp://ftp.gwdg.de/pub/linux/misc/ncpfs⟩.

       nnffss    is the network filesystem used to access disks located on remote
              computers.

       nnttffss   is the filesystem native to  Microsoft  Windows  NT,  supporting
              features like ACLs, journaling, encryption, and so on.

       pprroocc   is  a  pseudo filesystem which is used as an interface to kernel
              data structures rather than reading and interpreting  _/_d_e_v_/_k_m_e_m.
              In particular, its files do not take disk space.  See pprroocc(5).

       RReeiisseerrffss
              is  a  journaling  filesystem, designed by Hans Reiser, that was
              integrated into Linux 2.4.1.

       ssmmbb    is a network filesystem that supports the SMB protocol, used  by
              Windows.  See ⟨https://www.samba.org/samba/smbfs/⟩.

       ssyyssvv   is  an  implementation  of  the System V/Coherent filesystem for
              Linux.  It implements all of Xenix FS, System V/386 FS, and  Co‐
              herent FS.

       uummssddooss is an extended DOS filesystem used by Linux.  It adds capability
              for  long  filenames,  UID/GID,  POSIX  permissions, and special
              files (devices, named pipes, etc.)  under  the  DOS  filesystem,
              without sacrificing compatibility with DOS.

       ttmmppffss  is  a filesystem whose contents reside in virtual memory.  Since
              the files on such filesystems typically reside in RAM, file  ac‐
              cess is extremely fast.  See ttmmppffss(5).

       vvffaatt   is  an  extended  FAT filesystem used by Microsoft Windows95 and
              Windows NT.  vvffaatt adds the capability to use long filenames  un‐
              der the MSDOS filesystem.

       XXFFSS    is  a  journaling  filesystem,  developed by SGI, that was inte‐
              grated into Linux 2.4.20.

       xxiiaaffss  was designed and implemented to be a stable, safe filesystem  by
              extending the Minix filesystem code.  It provides the basic most
              requested features without undue complexity.  The xxiiaaffss filesys‐
              tem  is  no longer actively developed or maintained.  It was re‐
              moved from the kernel in Linux 2.1.21.

